\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The fictitious charges of the rod and their images in the grounded plane (not to scale). Each image lies as far beyond the plane as its charge lies in front of it, at 
\begin_inset Formula $2d-z_{i}$
\end_inset
; 
\begin_inset Formula $P(z)$
\end_inset
 is an observation point on the axis.
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "fig:csm_images"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Reflecting in the grounded plane 
\begin_inset Formula $z=d$
\end_inset
 maps a position 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset
 to 
\begin_inset Formula $2d-z$
\end_inset
 and changes the sign of the charge:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the image of the point charge 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset
 at 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset
 is 
\begin_inset Formula $-q_{i}$
\end_inset
 at 
\begin_inset Formula $2d-z_{i}$
\end_inset
;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the image of the segment 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{j}$
\end_inset
 on 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[a_{j},b_{j}\right]$
\end_inset
 is 
\begin_inset Formula $-\lambda_{j}$
\end_inset
 on 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[2d-b_{j},\;2d-a_{j}\right]$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The images are not new unknowns: each one is tied to its real charge.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Step 3: The Contour Points (Equations)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The potential is imposed at 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset
 contour points 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(r_{k},z_{k}\right)$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $k=1,\dots,M$
\end_inset
, on the rod surface:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
on the hemisphere, one point per tip charge: 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(r_{k},z_{k}\right)=\left(R\sin(\theta_{k}),\;-R+R\cos(\theta_{k})\right)$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $k=1,\dots,N_{t}$
\end_inset
;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
on the shank, three points per segment at a quarter, half and three quarters of its length: 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(r_{k},z_{k}\right)=\left(R,\;a_{j}+f\,\ell_{j}\right)$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $f\in\left\{ \frac{1}{4},\frac{1}{2},\frac{3}{4}\right\}$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This gives 
\begin_inset Formula $M=N_{t}+3N_{s}$
\end_inset
 equations, more than the 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset
 unknowns.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Step 4: The Potential Coefficients
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The potential of a point charge 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset
 at 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(0,z_{q}\right)$
\end_inset
, seen at a point 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(r,z\right)$
\end_inset
, is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\varphi=\frac{q}{4\pi\epsilon_{r}\epsilon_{0}}\frac{1}{\sqrt{r^{2}+\left(z-z_{q}\right)^{2}}}\;\left[\mathrm{V}\right],\label{eq:csm_point_potential}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
and the potential of a uniform line charge 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset
 on the axis between 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $b$
\end_inset
 (
\begin_inset Formula $a<b$
\end_inset
) is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\varphi=\frac{\lambda}{4\pi\epsilon_{r}\epsilon_{0}}\ln\frac{F(b)}{F(a)}\;\left[\mathrm{V}\right],\qquad F(s)=\left(s-z\right)+\sqrt{\left(s-z\right)^{2}+r^{2}}\;\left[\mathrm{m}\right].\label{eq:csm_line_potential}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 
\series bold
potential coefficient
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{kn}$
\end_inset
 is the potential at contour point 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset
 produced by unknown 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset
 with unit value, together with its image. For a tip charge (
\begin_inset Formula $n=i$
\end_inset
):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P_{k,i}=\frac{1}{4\pi\epsilon_{r}\epsilon_{0}}\left[\frac{1}{\sqrt{r_{k}^{2}+\left(z_{k}-z_{i}\right)^{2}}}-\frac{1}{\sqrt{r_{k}^{2}+\left(z_{k}-2d+z_{i}\right)^{2}}}\right]\;\left[\mathrm{V/C}\right],\label{eq:csm_P_point}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
and for a shank segment (
\begin_inset Formula $n=N_{t}+j$
\end_inset
):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P_{k,N_{t}+j}=\frac{1}{4\pi\epsilon_{r}\epsilon_{0}}\left[\ln\frac{F_{k}(b_{j})}{F_{k}(a_{j})}-\ln\frac{F_{k}(2d-a_{j})}{F_{k}(2d-b_{j})}\right]\;\left[\mathrm{V\cdot m/C}\right],\qquad F_{k}(s)=\left(s-z_{k}\right)+\sqrt{\left(s-z_{k}\right)^{2}+r_{k}^{2}}\;\left[\mathrm{m}\right].\label{eq:csm_P_line}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In both cases the first term is the real charge and the second, with the minus sign, its image. The coefficients depend only on the geometry, not on the voltage.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Step 5: The System of Equations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By superposition, the potential at contour point 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset
 is the sum of the contributions of all unknowns, and it must equal the rod voltage:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\sum_{i=1}^{N_{t}}P_{k,i}\,q_{i}+\sum_{j=1}^{N_{s}}P_{k,N_{t}+j}\,\lambda_{j}=V\;\left[\mathrm{V}\right],\qquad k=1,\dots,M.\label{eq:csm_sum}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Written out for all contour points:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\begin{cases}
P_{1,1}q_{1}+\dots+P_{1,N_{t}}q_{N_{t}}+P_{1,N_{t}+1}\lambda_{1}+\dots+P_{1,N}\lambda_{N_{s}}=V\;\left[\mathrm{V}\right]\\
P_{2,1}q_{1}+\dots+P_{2,N_{t}}q_{N_{t}}+P_{2,N_{t}+1}\lambda_{1}+\dots+P_{2,N}\lambda_{N_{s}}=V\;\left[\mathrm{V}\right]\\
\qquad\vdots\\
P_{M,1}q_{1}+\dots+P_{M,N_{t}}q_{N_{t}}+P_{M,N_{t}+1}\lambda_{1}+\dots+P_{M,N}\lambda_{N_{s}}=V\;\left[\mathrm{V}\right]
\end{cases}\label{eq:csm_system}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
or, in matrix form,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\underbrace{\begin{bmatrix}P_{1,1} & \cdots & P_{1,N_{t}} & P_{1,N_{t}+1} & \cdots & P_{1,N}\\\vdots &  & \vdots & \vdots &  & \vdots\\P_{M,1} & \cdots & P_{M,N_{t}} & P_{M,N_{t}+1} & \cdots & P_{M,N}\end{bmatrix}}_{\substack{\mathbf{P}\;(M\times N)\\\left[\mathrm{V/C}\;\big|\;\mathrm{V\cdot m/C}\right]}}\underbrace{\begin{bmatrix}q_{1}\\\vdots\\q_{N_{t}}\\\lambda_{1}\\\vdots\\\lambda_{N_{s}}\end{bmatrix}}_{\substack{\mathbf{x}\\\left[\mathrm{C}\;\big|\;\mathrm{C/m}\right]}}=V\underbrace{\begin{bmatrix}1\\\vdots\\\vdots\\\vdots\\\vdots\\1\end{bmatrix}}_{\substack{\mathbf{1}\\\left[-\right]}}\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad\mathbf{P}\,\mathbf{x}=V\,\mathbf{1}\;\left[\mathrm{V}\right].\label{eq:csm_matrix}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The columns of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{P}$
\end_inset
 belong to the unknowns (first the tip charges, then the shank segments) and its rows to the contour points (first the hemisphere, then the shank). Since 
\begin_inset Formula $M>N$
\end_inset
 the system is overdetermined, and it is solved in the least-squares sense: the charges minimize the deviation of the surface potential from 
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset
 over all contour points,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\min_{\mathbf{x}}\left\Vert \mathbf{P}\,\mathbf{x}-V\,\mathbf{1}\right\Vert ^{2}\;\left[\mathrm{V^{2}}\right]\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad\mathbf{P}^{T}\mathbf{P}\,\mathbf{x}=V\,\mathbf{P}^{T}\mathbf{1}\;\left[\mathrm{V^{2}/C}\;\big|\;\mathrm{V^{2}\cdot m/C}\right].\label{eq:csm_least_squares}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This 
\begin_inset Formula $N\times N$
\end_inset
 linear system is solved numerically. Because 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{P}$
\end_inset
 depends only on the geometry, the solution is proportional to 
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset
: 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{x}=V\,\mathbf{x}_{1}$
\end_inset
, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{x}_{1}$
\end_inset
 is the solution for 
\begin_inset Formula $1\,\text{V}$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Step 6: The Applied Field on the Axis
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
With the charges known, the field on the axis (
\begin_inset Formula $r=0$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $0\le z<d$
\end_inset
) follows from the closed-form fields of point and line charges. Every real charge lies below the observation point and every image above it, and all contributions point towards the plane:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}E_{ext}(z)=\frac{1}{4\pi\epsilon_{r}\epsilon_{0}}\Bigg\{ & \sum_{i=1}^{N_{t}}q_{i}\Bigg[\underbrace{\frac{1}{\left(z-z_{i}\right)^{2}}}_{\text{point charge }q_{i}}+\underbrace{\frac{1}{\left(2d-z_{i}-z\right)^{2}}}_{\text{image }-q_{i}}\Bigg]\\
 & +\sum_{j=1}^{N_{s}}\lambda_{j}\Bigg[\underbrace{\frac{1}{z-b_{j}}-\frac{1}{z-a_{j}}}_{\text{line charge }\lambda_{j}}+\underbrace{\frac{1}{2d-b_{j}-z}-\frac{1}{2d-a_{j}-z}}_{\text{image }-\lambda_{j}}\Bigg]\Bigg\}\;\left[\mathrm{V/m}\right]\end{aligned}\label{eq:csm_field}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout
\begin_layout Standard
This is Coulomb's law in 
\series bold
discrete form
\series default
. For the space charge (Sections 2.3 to 2.5) the field is an integral of 
\begin_inset Formula $d\vec{E}=\frac{dq}{4\pi\epsilon_{r}\epsilon_{0}r^{2}}\hat{r}$
\end_inset
 over a continuous charge distribution. Here there is a finite number of charges, so the integral becomes a sum: each point charge contributes 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{q_{i}}{4\pi\epsilon_{r}\epsilon_{0}\,(z-z_{i})^{2}}$
\end_inset
, the inverse square of its distance from the observation point. For a line segment the integral of Coulomb's law along its length can be done in closed form, because 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{j}$
\end_inset
 is uniform:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\int_{a_{j}}^{b_{j}}\frac{\lambda_{j}\,dz'}{4\pi\epsilon_{r}\epsilon_{0}\left(z-z'\right)^{2}}=\frac{\lambda_{j}}{4\pi\epsilon_{r}\epsilon_{0}}\left[\frac{1}{z-b_{j}}-\frac{1}{z-a_{j}}\right]\;\left[\mathrm{V/m}\right],\label{eq:csm_segment_integral}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
which gives the brackets of the second sum; the image segments give the same expression with 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{j}$
\end_inset
 replaced by their mirror positions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since the charges are proportional to 
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset
, so is the field, 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{ext}(z,t)=V(t)\,e_{ext}(z)$
\end_inset
, which is used for the impulse in Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:impulse_voltage"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Accuracy
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The accuracy of the solution is checked by evaluating the potential at many points of the rod surface that were not used as contour points and comparing it with 
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset
. In this work 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{t}=30$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $g=1.2$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{rod}=5d$
\end_inset
; with these values the surface potential deviates from 
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset
 by well below 
\begin_inset Formula $1\%$
\end_inset
, and increasing 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{t}$
\end_inset
 does not change the field near the tip.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The rod length 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{rod}$
\end_inset
 is not given in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "wang1988"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and was assumed. It is not a numerical setting: a longer rod spreads its charge over more length and slightly lowers the field near the tip. Rod lengths between 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $8\,\text{m}$
\end_inset
 change the field at the tip by about 
\begin_inset Formula $4\%$
\end_inset
 and the field at the head of the streamer by about 
\begin_inset Formula $2.5\%$
\end_inset
, which does not change any of the conclusions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Newpage pagebreak
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Derivation of Space Charge Amplitude 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:amplitude_A"

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The axial space charge electric field 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{E}_{sp}(z)$
\end_inset

 is given by:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\vec{E}_{sp}(z)=\frac{1}{2\epsilon_{r}\epsilon_{0}}\int^{\frac{\theta_{B}}{2}}_{0}\int^{L_{cs}}_{0}\frac{\rho(r)r^{2}\sin\theta}{r^{3}_{1}}\left[z-r\cos\theta\right]dr\,d\theta\,\hat{z}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{1}=\sqrt{\left[z-r\cos\theta\right]^{2}+\left[r\sin\theta\right]^{2}}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho(r)=\frac{A}{(r+C)^{B}}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Volumetric Integration for 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{cs}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Integrating 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho(r)$
\end_inset

 over the conical volume 
\begin_inset Formula $dV=r^{2}\sin\theta\,dr\,d\theta\,d\phi$
\end_inset

:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
Q_{cs}=\int\int\int\rho(r)\;dv=\int^{2\pi}_{0}d\phi\int^{\frac{\theta_{B}}{2}}_{0}\sin\theta\,d\theta\int^{L_{cs}}_{0}\frac{A}{(r+C)^{B}}r{}^{2}\,dr
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Evaluating the angular terms:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
Q_{cs}=A\cdot2\pi\left[1-\cos\left(\frac{\theta_{B}}{2}\right)\right]\int^{L_{cs}}_{0}\frac{r^{2}}{(r+C)^{B}}\,dr
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Gauss's Law at Streamer Boundary
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Applying Gauss's Law over a closed surface 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 enclosing the streamer volume:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\oint_{S}\vec{E}\cdot d\vec{A}=\frac{Q_{cs}}{\epsilon_{0}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Assuming the flux is concentrated across the circular head area 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{\text{head}}$
\end_inset

 at axial distance 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{cs}$
\end_inset

 where the field magnitude is uniform (
\begin_inset Formula $\|\vec{E}\|=E_{c}$
\end_inset

):
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\iint_{S_{\text{head}}}E_{c}\,dA=E_{c}A_{\text{head}}=\frac{Q_{cs}}{\epsilon_{0}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
From cone trigonometry,
 the head radius is 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{\text{head}}=L_{cs}\tan\left(\frac{\theta_{B}}{2}\right)$
\end_inset

,
 giving the head area:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
A_{\text{head}}=\pi r^{2}_{\text{head}}=\pi\left[L_{cs}\tan\left(\frac{\theta_{B}}{2}\right)\right]^{2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Substituting 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{\text{head}}$
\end_inset

 back into Gauss's Law and isolating 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{cs}$
\end_inset

:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
Q_{cs}=\epsilon_{0}E_{c}A_{\text{head}}=\pi\epsilon_{0}E_{c}L^{2}_{cs}\tan^{2}\left(\frac{\theta_{B}}{2}\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Equating and Isolating 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Equating both expressions for 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{cs}$
\end_inset

 yields the explicit result:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
A(E_{c},B,C,L_{cs},\theta_{B})=\frac{\overbrace{\pi\epsilon_{0}E_{c}L^{2}_{cs}\tan^{2}\left(\frac{\theta_{B}}{2}\right)}^{Q_{cs}}}{2\pi\left(1-\cos\left(\frac{\theta_{B}}{2}\right)\right)\int^{L_{cs}}_{0}\frac{r^{2}}{(r+C)^{B}}\,dr}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Newpage pagebreak
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Summarizing
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:static_summary"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote
Space Charge Density Function & Domains:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\rho(r)=\frac{A}{\left(r+C\right)^{B}}\;\;\left[\frac{C}{m^{3}}\right]\;\;,\;\;\text{Domains:}\begin{cases}
r\in\left[0\;,\;L_{cs}\right]\\
\theta\in\left[0\;,\;\theta_{B}/2\right]\\
\phi\in[0\;,\;2\pi]
\end{cases}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
A(\;E_{c}\;,\;B\;,\;C\;,\;L_{cs}\;,\;\theta_{B}\;)=\frac{\overbrace{\pi\epsilon_{0}E_{c}L^{2}_{cs}\tan^{2}\left(\frac{\theta_{B}}{2}\right)}^{Q_{cs}}}{2\pi\left[1-\cos\left(\frac{\theta_{B}}{2}\right)\right]\int^{L_{cs}}_{0}\frac{r^{2}}{(r+C)^{B}}\,dr}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
B,C are choosen such that 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{total}=E_{ext}+E_{sp}+E_{I_{rod}}+E_{I_{plane}}=\text{constant}$
\end_inset

 (uniform),
 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{c}:$
\end_inset

 Average electric field in the streamer region ( typically ~5.0 [kV/cm] ).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Space Charge (
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{E}_{sp}$
\end_inset

)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote
Space charge attenuation distance:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
r_{1}=\sqrt{\left[z-rcos(\theta)\right]{}^{2}+\left[rsin(\theta)\right]^{2}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote
Space charge electric field:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\vec{E}_{sp}=\frac{1}{2\epsilon_{r}\epsilon_{0}}\int^{\frac{\theta_{B}}{2}}_{0}\int^{L_{cs}}_{0}\frac{\rho(r)r^{2}sin(\theta)}{r^{3}_{1}}[z-rcos(\theta)]drd\theta\hat{z}\label{eq:final_Esp}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Rod Image (
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{E}_{I_{rod}}$
\end_inset

)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote
Distance between space charge & rod center.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
r_{2}=\sqrt{\left[R+rcos(\theta)\right]^{2}+\left[rsin(\theta)\right]^{2}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote
Distance of the rod image charge from its center:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
r_{3}=\frac{R^{2}}{r_{2}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote
Rod image charge attenuation distance:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
r_{4}=\sqrt{\left(z+R-\frac{r_{3}}{r_{2}}\left[R+rcos(\theta)\right]\right)^{2}+\left(\frac{r_{3}}{r_{2}}rsin(\theta)\right)^{2}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote
Rod image electric field:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\vec{E}_{I_{rod}}=-\frac{1}{2\epsilon_{r}\epsilon_{0}}\int^{\frac{\theta_{B}}{2}}_{0}\int^{L_{cs}}_{0}\frac{\rho(r)r^{2}Rsin(\theta)}{r^{3}_{4}r_{2}}\left[z+R-\frac{r_{3}}{r_{2}}\left(R+rcos(\theta)\right)\right]drd\theta\hat{z}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Plane Image (
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{E}_{I_{plane}}$
\end_inset

)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote
Plane image charge attenuation distance:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
r_{5}=\sqrt{\left[rsin(\theta)\right]^{2}+\left(2d-rcos(\theta)-z\right)^{2}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote
Plane image electric field:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\vec{E}_{I_{plane}}=\frac{1}{2\epsilon_{r}\epsilon_{0}}\int^{\frac{\theta_{B}}{2}}_{0}\int^{L_{cs}}_{0}\frac{\rho(r)r^{2}sin(\theta)}{r^{3}_{5}}\left(2d-rcos(\theta)-z\right)drd\theta\hat{z}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Newpage pagebreak
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Mathematical Equivalence with the Wang–Haiting Formulation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To validate the analytical formulation developed above,
 we show that it is identical to the space-charge model published by Wang and Haiting 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "wang1988"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 (Eqs.
 4 and 5 of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "wang1988"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 While the Wang–Haiting equations use the intermediate angles 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{3}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{4}$
\end_inset

,
 our implementation uses coordinate-based algebraic forms that are better suited to numerical integration.
 This section proves,
 term by term,
 that the two are the same,
 and lists the typographical errors of the original paper that the derivation corrects.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Common Coordinate System
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Both formulations use the same coordinate system,
 so no transformation between them is needed:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the origin 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{O}$
\end_inset

 is the apex (tip) of the rod electrode,
 which is also the apex of the space-charge cone;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 axis is the axis of symmetry,
 pointing from the rod towards the grounded plane;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the centre of the hemispherical rod tip lies at 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{C}=-R\hat{z}$
\end_inset

 and the plane at 
\begin_inset Formula $z=d$
\end_inset

,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $d$
\end_inset

 is the apex-to-plane gap distance.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The axial coordinate 
\begin_inset Formula $Z$
\end_inset

 of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "wang1988"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 is therefore our 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon=\epsilon_{r}\epsilon_{0}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Direct Space-Charge Field
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
From 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:final_Esp"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

,
 the direct space-charge field on the axis is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\vec{E}_{sp}(z)=\frac{1}{2\epsilon_{r}\epsilon_{0}}\int^{\theta_{B}/2}_{0}\int^{L_{cs}}_{0}\frac{\rho(r)\,r^{2}\sin(\theta)}{r^{3}_{1}}\left[z-r\cos(\theta)\right]dr\,d\theta\,\hat{z},\qquad r_{1}=\sqrt{\left[z-r\cos(\theta)\right]^{2}+\left[r\sin(\theta)\right]^{2}}.
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Taking the minus sign out of the bracket,
 
\begin_inset Formula $z-r\cos(\theta)=-\left[r\cos(\theta)-z\right]$
\end_inset

,
 and noting that the square in 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{1}$
\end_inset

 does not change,
 gives
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E_{sp}=-\frac{1}{2\epsilon}\int^{\theta_{B}/2}_{0}\int^{L_{cs}}_{0}\frac{\rho(r)\,r^{2}\sin(\theta)}{r^{3}_{1}}\left[r\cos(\theta)-Z\right]dr\,d\theta,\qquad r_{1}=\sqrt{\left[r\cos(\theta)-Z\right]^{2}+\left[r\sin(\theta)\right]^{2}},
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
which is Eq.
 4 of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "wang1988"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote

\series bold
Note on a typographical error in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "wang1988"
literal "false"

\end_inset

:

\series default
 the paper prints the second term of 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{1}$
\end_inset

 as 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[r_{3}\sin(\theta)\right]^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $r\sin(\theta)$
\end_inset

 is the distance of the charge element from the axis,
 the correct term is 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[r\sin(\theta)\right]^{2}$
\end_inset

,
 as derived above.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Rod Image Field
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
From 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:Erod_theta3_theta4"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

,
 the rod image field is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\vec{E}_{I_{rod}}(z)=-\frac{1}{2\epsilon_{r}\epsilon_{0}}\int^{\theta_{B}/2}_{0}\int^{L_{cs}}_{0}\frac{R\,r^{2}\rho(r)\sin(\theta)\cos(\theta_{4})}{r^{2}_{4}\,r_{2}}dr\,d\theta\,\hat{z},
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
whose integrand is the same as in Eq.
 5 of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "wang1988"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 It remains to show that the auxiliary quantities are the same.
 The first three are identical by construction,
 because 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{r}_{2}=\vec{P}_{dq_{sp}}-\vec{C}$
\end_inset

 is measured from the centre of the rod tip in both formulations:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
r_{2}=\sqrt{\left[R+r\cos(\theta)\right]^{2}+\left[r\sin(\theta)\right]^{2}},\qquad r_{3}=\frac{R^{2}}{r_{2}},\qquad\theta_{3}=\sin^{-1}\left(\frac{r\sin(\theta)}{r_{2}}\right).
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{4}$
\end_inset

,
 the image charge lies at 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{P}_{dq'}=\vec{C}+r_{3}\hat{r}_{2}$
\end_inset

,
 so its axial distance from the observer is 
\begin_inset Formula $z+R-r_{3}\cos(\theta_{3})$
\end_inset

 and its distance from the axis is 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{3}\sin(\theta_{3})$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
r_{4}=\sqrt{\left[Z+R-r_{3}\cos(\theta_{3})\right]^{2}+\left[r_{3}\sin(\theta_{3})\right]^{2}},
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
which is the 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{4}$
\end_inset

 of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "wang1988"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 The term 
\begin_inset Formula $+R$
\end_inset

 is the distance between the origin (apex) and the centre of the rod tip,
 about which the Kelvin image is constructed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote

\series bold
Note on a typographical error in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "wang1988"
literal "false"

\end_inset

:

\series default
 the paper prints the second term of 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{4}$
\end_inset

 as 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[r_{3}\sin(\theta)\right]^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 The image lies on the line from 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{C}$
\end_inset

 to the charge element,
 at angle 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{3}$
\end_inset

 from the axis,
 so the correct term is 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[r_{3}\sin(\theta_{3})\right]^{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Finally,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "wang1988"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 defines 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{4}=\sin^{-1}\left(r_{3}\sin(\theta_{3})/r_{4}\right)$
\end_inset

.
 The inverse sine returns 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{4}\in\left[0,\pi/2\right]$
\end_inset

,
 so it always gives 
\begin_inset Formula $\cos(\theta_{4})\ge0$
\end_inset

,
 whereas our derivation gives the signed axial projection
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\cos(\theta_{4})=\frac{\vec{r}_{4}\cdot\hat{z}}{r_{4}}=\frac{z+R-r_{3}\cos(\theta_{3})}{r_{4}}.
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The two agree because this projection is never negative in the gap.
 Every charge element lies outside the rod,
 so 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{2}\ge R$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{3}=R^{2}/r_{2}\le R$
\end_inset

:
 the image lies inside the hemisphere.
 Hence,
 for every observation point 
\begin_inset Formula $z\ge0$
\end_inset

,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
r_{3}\cos(\theta_{3})\le r_{3}\le R\le z+R\quad\Rightarrow\quad z+R-r_{3}\cos(\theta_{3})\ge0,
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
and the inverse-sine definition of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "wang1988"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and our signed projection give the same 
\begin_inset Formula $\cos(\theta_{4})$
\end_inset

.
 The coordinate form of the conclusion of the rod image subsection,
 which evaluates 
\begin_inset Formula $z+R-\frac{r_{3}}{r_{2}}\left[R+r\cos(\theta)\right]$
\end_inset

 directly,
 is therefore identical to Eq.
 5 of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "wang1988"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 without evaluating 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{3}$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{4}$
\end_inset

 during the integration.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Grounded Plane Image Field and Boundary Approximations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In long rod-plane gaps (
\begin_inset Formula $d\gg R$
\end_inset

) the ionization and streamer propagation occur near the rod tip.
 Wang and Haiting omit the image of the space charge in the grounded plane (
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{E}_{I_{plane}}\approx0$
\end_inset

),
 so their total field is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\vec{E}_{\text{total, WH}}=\vec{E}_{ex}+\vec{E}_{sp}+\vec{E}_{I_{rod}}.
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Our model keeps 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{E}_{I_{plane}}$
\end_inset

 to satisfy the boundary condition on the plane over the whole gap.
 Near the rod (
\begin_inset Formula $z\ll d$
\end_inset

) the image charges lie about 
\begin_inset Formula $2d$
\end_inset

 away and 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{E}_{I_{plane}}\to0$
\end_inset

,
 so the two formulations agree where 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "wang1988"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 applies.
\end_layout

