\begin_layout Standard
The three conditions that define the waveform are:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Crest position:
\series default
 at 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{cr}$
\end_inset
 the slope of 
\begin_inset Formula $v(t)$
\end_inset
 is zero, 
\begin_inset Formula $\dot{v}(T_{cr})=0$
\end_inset
;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Crest value:
\series default
 the voltage there equals 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{p}$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $v(T_{cr})=1$
\end_inset
;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Half value:
\series default
 at 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{h}$
\end_inset
 the voltage is 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{p}/2$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $v(T_{h})=1/2$
\end_inset
, with 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{h}>T_{cr}$
\end_inset
 (on the tail).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the first condition, the derivative of 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:impulse_normalized"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
 keeps the constant 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset
 in front, and setting it to zero at the crest gives
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\dot{v}(T_{cr})=k\left(-\alpha\,e^{-\alpha T_{cr}}+\beta\,e^{-\beta T_{cr}}\right)=0\;\Rightarrow\;-\alpha\,e^{-\alpha T_{cr}}+\beta\,e^{-\beta T_{cr}}=0,\label{eq:impulse_crest_condition}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
since 
\begin_inset Formula $k\neq0$
\end_inset
 (otherwise 
\begin_inset Formula $v(t)=0$
\end_inset
 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset
) and both sides can be divided by 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset
. Physically, 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset
 only stretches the curve vertically: it sets how high the crest is, not when it occurs. The crest time therefore depends on 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset
 alone, and 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset
 is fixed by the second condition.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In equation form, with the unknowns 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset
:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\begin{cases}
-\alpha\,e^{-\alpha T_{cr}}+\beta\,e^{-\beta T_{cr}}=0\\
k\left(e^{-\alpha T_{cr}}-e^{-\beta T_{cr}}\right)=1\\
k\left(e^{-\alpha T_{h}}-e^{-\beta T_{h}}\right)=\dfrac{1}{2}
\end{cases}\label{eq:impulse_system}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The unknowns appear inside exponentials, so the system is non-linear and has no closed-form solution. In vector form, with the vector of unknowns 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{x}=\left[\alpha,\;\beta,\;k\right]^{T}$
\end_inset
, it reads
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{F}(\mathbf{x})=\begin{bmatrix}-\alpha\,e^{-\alpha T_{cr}}+\beta\,e^{-\beta T_{cr}}\\k\left(e^{-\alpha T_{cr}}-e^{-\beta T_{cr}}\right)-1\\k\left(e^{-\alpha T_{h}}-e^{-\beta T_{h}}\right)-\frac{1}{2}\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}0\\0\\0\end{bmatrix}.\label{eq:impulse_vector}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The system is solved numerically in Python (double_exponential_parameters in solver/utilities.py).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
The Applied Field During the Impulse
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The applied field is the Laplacian field of the electrodes, so it is proportional to the applied voltage. If 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{ex}(z)$
\end_inset
 is the applied field for 
\begin_inset Formula $1\,\text{V}$
\end_inset
 on the rod, then at any instant
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E_{ex}(z,t)=V(t)\,e_{ex}(z).\label{eq:applied_field_linear}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Charge Simulation Method is therefore solved only once, for 
\begin_inset Formula $1\,\text{V}$
\end_inset
, and its result is scaled by 
\begin_inset Formula $V(t)$
\end_inset
 at every time step. The space charge fields do not depend on the voltage directly: they change only through the drift, whose speed depends on 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{ex}(z_{c},t)$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Successive Corona Bursts
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:bursts"

\end_inset

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So far the model follows one cloud, the space charge of the first corona streamer. During an impulse the rod emits several streamers (bursts), one after the other, and the space charge grows with every burst. This section extends the model to any number of bursts. 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "fig:burst_geometry"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
 shows the clouds at the instant of a burst, and 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "fig:burst_timeline"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
 shows how the bursts follow each other in time.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Two Time Scales
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A streamer of length 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{cs}$
\end_inset
 grows at the streamer velocity 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{s}$
\end_inset
, in the time 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau_{s}=L_{cs}/v_{s}$
\end_inset
, while the ions drift at 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{d}=\mu^{+}E$
\end_inset
. Because 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{s}\gg v_{d}$
\end_inset
, the ions hardly move while a streamer grows:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
v_{d}\,\tau_{s}=\frac{\mu^{+}E}{v_{s}}\,L_{cs}\ll L_{cs}.\label{eq:time_scales}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A burst is therefore modelled as instantaneous: its cone appears at once, with its apex at the rod tip, and only afterwards drifts. The real growth of the space charge, a cone that keeps its apex at the tip and becomes larger, is represented by a sequence of such cones.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
The Clouds
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Burst 
\begin_inset Formula $k=1,2,\dots$
\end_inset
 happens at the instant 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{k}$
\end_inset
 and leaves a cloud with its own length 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{k}$
\end_inset
, the common cone angle 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{B}$
\end_inset
, and the charge 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{k}$
\end_inset
 and amplitude 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{k}$
\end_inset
 of Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:amplitude_A"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\rho_{k}(r)=\frac{A_{k}}{(r+C)^{B}},\qquad Q_{k}=\pi\epsilon_{0}E_{c}L_{k}^{2}\tan^{2}\left(\theta_{B}/2\right),\qquad A_{k}=\frac{Q_{k}}{2\pi\left[1-\cos\left(\theta_{B}/2\right)\right]\int_{0}^{L_{k}}\frac{r^{2}}{(r+C)^{B}}\,dr}.\label{eq:burst_cloud}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Each cloud drifts as a rigid body by 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{d,k}(t)$
\end_inset
, with 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{d,k}(t)=0$
\end_inset
 for 
\begin_inset Formula $t\le t_{k}$
\end_inset
. Its field is the sum of the three space-charge fields of Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:drift_image_fields"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
, evaluated with 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{k}$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{k}$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{d,k}(t)$
\end_inset
:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E_{k}(z,t)=E_{sp,k}(z,t)+E_{I_{rod},k}(z,t)+E_{I_{plane},k}(z,t).\label{eq:cloud_field}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
The Total Space Charge and the Recursive Total Field
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Float figure
placement H
alignment document
wide false
sideways false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename assets/v2/burst_geometry.pdf
	width 95col%

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The burst model at the instant 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{3}$
\end_inset
 of the third burst. The new cloud (solid) has its apex at the rod tip and the length 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{3}$
\end_inset
 at which the field at its head is 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{c}$
\end_inset
. The history clouds (dashed) have drifted by 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{d,1}$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{d,2}$
\end_inset
; each moves with the velocity 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{v}_{k}$
\end_inset
 at its own point 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{c,k}$
\end_inset
. Right: the part of a cloud beyond the plane is absorbed, so its radial limit becomes 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{max}$
\end_inset
.
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "fig:burst_geometry"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The space charge in the gap is the sum of every cloud created so far, each shifted by its own drift (
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_{k}(\vec{x})$
\end_inset
 is the density of cloud 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset
 with its apex at the rod tip):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\rho(\vec{x},t)=\sum_{k:\,t_{k}\le t}\rho_{k}\left(\vec{x}-z_{d,k}(t)\,\hat{z}\right),\qquad Q(t)=\sum_{k:\,t_{k}\le t}Q_{k}.\label{eq:total_density}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The fields are linear in the charge, so the total field is the applied field plus the field of every cloud:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E_{total}(z,t)=V(t)\,e_{ex}(z)+\sum_{k:\,t_{k}\le t}E_{k}(z,t).\label{eq:total_field_bursts}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For burst 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset
, the clouds of the earlier bursts form its 
\series bold
history
\series default
:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
H_{k}(z,t)=\sum_{j<k}E_{j}(z,t),\qquad H_{1}(z,t)=0,\label{eq:history}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
which grows by one cloud at every burst,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
H_{k+1}(z,t)=H_{k}(z,t)+E_{k}(z,t).\label{eq:history_recursion}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Between the bursts 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $k+1$
\end_inset
 the total field is therefore 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{total}=V(t)\,e_{ex}(z)+H_{k}(z,t)+E_{k}(z,t)=V(t)\,e_{ex}(z)+H_{k+1}(z,t)$
\end_inset
: every new burst is computed on top of the drifting history of the earlier ones.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Burst Condition
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Float figure
placement H
alignment document
wide false
sideways false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename assets/v2/burst_timeline.pdf
	width 95col%

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The recursion in time (schematic). The tip field rises with 
\begin_inset Formula $V(t)$
\end_inset
 until it reaches 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{inc}$
\end_inset
. At each burst a cloud is added to the history and the tip field drops; it then recovers because the voltage rises and the clouds drift away.
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "fig:burst_timeline"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The rod tip and the air do not change, so every burst needs the same tip field as the first one, 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{inc}=V_{inc}\,e_{ex}(0)$
\end_inset
. Burst 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset
 happens at the first instant after the previous burst at which the total field at the tip reaches it:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
t_{k}=\min\left\{ t>t_{k-1}\,:\;V(t)\,e_{ex}(0)+H_{k}(0,t)=E_{inc}\right\} ,\qquad E_{inc}=V_{inc}\,e_{ex}(0).\label{eq:burst_condition}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For 
\begin_inset Formula $k=1$
\end_inset
 the history is zero and the condition reduces to 
\begin_inset Formula $V(t_{1})=V_{inc}$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Growth of the New Cloud
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
During the burst the older clouds stay where they are, at 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{d,j}(t_{k})$
\end_inset
, because of 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:time_scales"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
. The streamer keeps growing while the field at its head is above 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{c}$
\end_inset
. Its length 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{k}$
\end_inset
 is therefore the length beyond which the head field stays below 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{c}$
\end_inset
, the 
\series bold
largest
\series default
 root of the head condition:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
L_{k}=\max\left\{ L\,:\;V(t_{k})\,e_{ex}(L)+E_{k}(L,t_{k})+H_{k}(L,t_{k})=E_{c}\right\} ,\label{eq:burst_length}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{k}(L,t_{k})$
\end_inset
 is the field of the new, not yet drifted cloud of length 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset
 at its own head; its charge and amplitude depend on 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset
 through 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:burst_cloud"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
. This is one equation for the single unknown 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{k}$
\end_inset
, solved by root finding.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The largest root, and not the first one, is used because of the history. Close to the tip the head field of the new streamer passes the drifted apex of an older cloud, which is the densest part of that cloud (
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_{j}$
\end_inset
 is largest at 
\begin_inset Formula $r=0$
\end_inset
). Its field makes a short, narrow dip in the head field, and the streamer is assumed to cross it. The dip is sharp only because the cloud keeps its shape during the drift; a real cloud spreads out near its apex.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the first burst (
\begin_inset Formula $H_{1}=0$
\end_inset
) this is the cone-angle condition of Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:static_summary"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
 with the roles swapped: there 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{cs}$
\end_inset
 is given and 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{B}$
\end_inset
 is the unknown. The cone angle is therefore 
\series bold
calibrated once
\series default
, on a first streamer of known length, and kept for every later burst and every impulse. Every length, 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{1}$
\end_inset
 included, then follows from 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:burst_length"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Drift of Many Clouds
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Each cloud moves with the ion velocity at its own reference point 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{c,k}$
\end_inset
, driven by every field except its own direct field:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E_{sp,k}(z_{c,k,0},t_{k})=0,\qquad z_{c,k}(t)=z_{c,k,0}+z_{d,k}(t),\label{eq:burst_zc}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{dz_{d,k}}{dt}=\mu^{+}\Big[V(t)\,e_{ex}(z_{c,k})+E_{I_{rod},k}(z_{c,k},t)+E_{I_{plane},k}(z_{c,k},t)+\sum_{j\neq k}E_{j}(z_{c,k},t)\Big],\qquad z_{d,k}(t_{k})=0.\label{eq:burst_drift}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The clouds are coupled: each one is pushed by the fields of the others. The drifts are therefore one system of ordinary differential equations, integrated together, which gains one equation at every burst. Clouds may overlap; by 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:total_density"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
 their densities simply add.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Absorption by the Plane
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A charge element of cloud 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset
 sits at 
\begin_inset Formula $r\cos(\theta)+z_{d,k}(t)$
\end_inset
. It reaches the grounded plane and is neutralized when
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
r\cos(\theta)+z_{d,k}(t)\ge d.\label{eq:absorption_element}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The field integrals then run only over the charge that is still in the gap. The upper radial limit becomes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
r_{max,k}(\theta,t)=\min\left[L_{k},\;\frac{d-z_{d,k}(t)}{\cos(\theta)}\right],\label{eq:absorption_limit}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
so the charge of the cloud decreases:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
Q_{k}(t)=2\pi\int_{0}^{\theta_{B}/2}\int_{0}^{r_{max,k}(\theta,t)}\rho_{k}(r)\,r^{2}\sin(\theta)\,dr\,d\theta.\label{eq:absorption_charge}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The cloud is completely absorbed, and removed, when its apex reaches the plane:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
z_{d,k}(t)\ge d.\label{eq:absorption_cloud}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
With an average field of about 
\begin_inset Formula $V/d$
\end_inset
 in the gap, the time an ion needs to cross it is of the order
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
t_{cross}\approx\frac{d}{\mu^{+}\,V/d}=\frac{d^{2}}{\mu^{+}V}.\label{eq:crossing_time}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{cross}$
\end_inset
 is much longer than the impulse, no cloud reaches the plane while the voltage is applied.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
End of the Simulation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The simulation covers a time window 
\begin_inset Formula $0\le t\le t_{end}$
\end_inset
. Here the bursts are searched on the front of the impulse, 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{end}=T_{cr}$
\end_inset
:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
after the crest 
\begin_inset Formula $dV/dt<0$
\end_inset
, so the applied field at the tip falls and a new burst can only come from the clouds drifting away;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
in long gaps the streamers on the front lead to the inception of a leader, which the model does not include. The model describes the corona bursts before the leader.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
The Recursive Algorithm
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Calibrate 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{B}$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{inc}$
\end_inset
 on a first streamer of known 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{inc}$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{cs}$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Start with no clouds: 
\begin_inset Formula $k=1$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{1}=0$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Integrate the drift 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:burst_drift"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
 of the existing clouds forward in time until the burst condition 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:burst_condition"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
 is met, a cloud reaches the plane (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:absorption_cloud"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
, remove it and continue), or 
\begin_inset Formula $t=t_{end}$
\end_inset
 (stop).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
At the burst: solve 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:burst_length"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
 for 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{k}$
\end_inset
, compute 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{k}$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{k}$
\end_inset
 from 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:burst_cloud"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{c,k,0}$
\end_inset
 from 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:burst_zc"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
, add the cloud to the history with 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:history_recursion"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
, set 
\begin_inset Formula $k\to k+1$
\end_inset
 and go back to step 3.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Newpage pagebreak
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Summary of the Mathematical Framework
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This section collects the complete model of Sections 2 to 4 in one place. Compared with the summary of Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:static_summary"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

, the drift of the space charge and the time-dependent voltage are now built into every equation, so the formulas below give the field at any position 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset
 and any time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset
. All distances are measured from the rod tip apex, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon=\epsilon_{r}\epsilon_{0}$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

