\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Near the tip
\series default
 the space charge opposes the applied field. At the apex 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{sp}=-29.1\,\text{kV/cm}$
\end_inset
 and the rod image adds 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{I_{rod}}=-123.8\,\text{kV/cm}$
\end_inset
, so the total field drops from 
\begin_inset Formula $149.4\,\text{kV/cm}$
\end_inset
 (applied field alone) to 
\begin_inset Formula $-3.6\,\text{kV/cm}$
\end_inset
. No new ionization can start at the tip.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
The direct field changes sign
\series default
 at 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{c}=0.572\,\text{cm}$
\end_inset
. Beyond this point the space charge strengthens the field towards the plane.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
In the streamer region
\series default
 the total field stays close to 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{c}=5\,\text{kV/cm}$
\end_inset
, as required by condition (b) of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "wang1988"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
The plane image
\series default
 is negligible near the tip, below 
\begin_inset Formula $10^{-3}\,\text{kV/cm}$
\end_inset
, because the image charges lie about 
\begin_inset Formula $160\,\text{cm}$
\end_inset
 away.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Why the tip field is negative.
\series default
 The total field at the apex, 
\begin_inset Formula $-3.6\,\text{kV/cm}$
\end_inset
, is the difference of two large numbers: the applied field gives 
\begin_inset Formula $+149.4\,\text{kV/cm}$
\end_inset
, and the space charge together with its rod image gives 
\begin_inset Formula $-152.9\,\text{kV/cm}$
\end_inset
 (see 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "fig:repro_fig3_all"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
). The difference is 
\begin_inset Formula $2.4\,\%$
\end_inset
 of the applied field. The model fixes the charge and 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{B}$
\end_inset
 only at the head of the streamer, never at the tip, so nothing holds the tip field at zero, and small changes near the apex (in 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{B}$
\end_inset
 or the applied field) can change its sign.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In a real gap the field at the anode cannot stay negative. A negative field means that the potential just in front of the tip is higher than 
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset
, so the positive ions between the tip and that maximum drift back into the rod and are neutralized, until the field returns to about zero. The model does not contain this, because the cloud keeps its charge and moves as a rigid body. The value 
\begin_inset Formula $-3.6\,\text{kV/cm}$
\end_inset
 should therefore be read as “about zero”: far below the inception field, which is what matters for the next streamer. After a drift of 
\begin_inset Formula $0.07\,\text{cm}$
\end_inset
 the tip field becomes 
\begin_inset Formula $+42.7\,\text{kV/cm}$
\end_inset
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "tab:repro_fig4_tip"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
).
\end_layout

