\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Our 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{sp}$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{I_{rod}}$
\end_inset
 compared with the curves digitized from Figure 3 of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "wang1988"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 (subscript wh). The dashed line marks the streamer field 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{c}=5\,\text{kV/cm}$
\end_inset
; the vertical dashed lines mark where each 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{sp}$
\end_inset
 peaks.
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "fig:repro_fig3_compare"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
What agrees.
\series default
 Both models give the same picture: 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{sp}$
\end_inset
 is strongly negative at the tip, changes sign about half a centimetre in front of it and stays positive along the streamer, while 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{I_{rod}}$
\end_inset
 is negative and confined close to the rod.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
What differs.
\series default
 Three differences can be seen in the figure:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
the paper's 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{sp}$
\end_inset
 changes sign further from the tip than ours;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
the paper's 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{I_{rod}}$
\end_inset
 reaches further into the gap than ours;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
the paper's 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{sp}$
\end_inset
 reaches its maximum, about 
\begin_inset Formula $5.3\,\text{kV/cm}$
\end_inset
, at about 
\begin_inset Formula $z=2.3\,\text{cm}$
\end_inset
 and then falls quickly, while ours reaches its maximum at the head of the streamer, 
\begin_inset Formula $z=L_{cs}=2.7\,\text{cm}$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The third difference can be explained without any calculation. In the model the charge ends at the head of the cone. Moving along the axis towards the head, more and more of the charge lies behind the observation point and pushes towards the plane, while less and less lies in front of it and pushes back. At the head all the charge is behind, and beyond it the field can only decrease. The model therefore always has its maximum at the head, as ours does.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The paper's maximum also shows how the paper applies its condition. The total field is the sum of all components,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E_{total}=E_{ex}+E_{sp}+E_{I_{rod}}+E_{I_{plane}},\label{eq:repro_total_sum}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
and the paper only asks for it to be about 
\begin_inset Formula $5\,\text{kV/cm}$
\end_inset
 in the streamer region, without saying at which point. Our model imposes it exactly at the head, 
\begin_inset Formula $z=L_{cs}$
\end_inset
. There the applied field gives about 
\begin_inset Formula $1\,\text{kV/cm}$
\end_inset
 and the image fields are very small (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "tab:repro_head_fields"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
), so 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{sp}$
\end_inset
 can only reach about 
\begin_inset Formula $4\,\text{kV/cm}$
\end_inset
. Adding the paper's 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{sp}$
\end_inset
 to our other components gives an estimate of the paper's total field (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "tab:repro_implied_total"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
). It is about 
\begin_inset Formula $5.2\,\text{kV/cm}$
\end_inset
 at the head, close to our condition, but about 
\begin_inset Formula $6.4\,\text{kV/cm}$
\end_inset
 at 
\begin_inset Formula $z=2.3\,\text{cm}$
\end_inset
, where the paper's 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{sp}$
\end_inset
 peaks. The estimate is approximate, because it uses our applied and image fields.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The paper's curve is therefore not wrong: it keeps the field at about 
\begin_inset Formula $5\,\text{kV/cm}$
\end_inset
 in a looser sense, with a less uniform field than ours, and the paper itself accepts 
\begin_inset Formula $4$
\end_inset
 to 
\begin_inset Formula $5.5\,\text{kV/cm}$
\end_inset
 in its Figure 5 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "wang1988"
literal "false"

\end_inset
. It is, however, not consistent with an exact condition at the head, which is the one our model uses.
\end_layout



