\begin_inset Formula $5\,\text{kV/cm}$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Conclusion.
\series default
 Our implementation follows the paper's equations (Section 3) and reproduces the shape and the main features of Figure 3, but not its exact values. The paper does not say where it applies its condition of about 
\begin_inset Formula $5\,\text{kV/cm}$
\end_inset
: its curve satisfies it loosely along the streamer, while we impose it exactly at the head. This, or another detail of the authors' calculation that the paper does not report, most likely explains the differences. The reproduction is therefore considered qualitatively validated.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Figure 4: Drift of the Space Charge
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
After the first streamer the space charge drifts slowly towards the plane (Section 4). Figure 4 of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "wang1988"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 shows 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{sp}$
\end_inset
 before and after such a drift, but the paper does not say how long the drift lasted. In its figure the minimum of the drifted curve, where the apex of the moved cloud now sits, is at about 
\begin_inset Formula $z=0.07\,\text{cm}$
\end_inset
, so we moved the cloud of Figure 3 by 
\begin_inset Formula $0.07\,\text{cm}$
\end_inset
. With the paper's ion mobility our solver finds that this takes 
\begin_inset Formula $12.5\,\mu\text{s}$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

