\begin_layout Subsection
Figure 5: The Field During a Switching Impulse
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Figure 5 of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "wang1988"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 shows the field near the tip while a switching impulse rises: (a) without and (b) with the space charge of the first streamer. The impulse is the double exponential of Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:impulse_voltage"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
, fitted to a crest of 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{p}=320\,\text{kV}$
\end_inset
 at 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{cr}=100\,\mu\text{s}$
\end_inset
. The paper does not give the tail, so the standard switching-impulse value 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{h}=2500\,\mu\text{s}$
\end_inset
 was assumed. The fit gives 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha=291.1\,\text{s}^{-1}$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta=52\,177\,\text{s}^{-1}$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $k=1.035$
\end_inset
 (Parameters sheet of the Figure 5 workbook). The simulation runs as follows:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
until 
\begin_inset Formula $V(t)$
\end_inset
 reaches the inception voltage 
\begin_inset Formula $27.5\,\text{kV}$
\end_inset
 (at 
\begin_inset Formula $t=1.67\,\mu\text{s}$
\end_inset
) there is no space charge, and the field is the applied field alone;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
at that instant the space charge of Figure 3 is created;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
afterwards the voltage keeps rising while the cloud drifts, and at each time step the field is the applied field plus the field of the drifted cloud.
\end_layout

