\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
First streamer.
\series default
 Its curves are flat (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "fig:repro_fig6"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
 to 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "fig:repro_fig8"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
). The first burst needs the fixed tip field 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{inc}$
\end_inset
, and the applied field is proportional to the voltage, so 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{inc}=E_{inc}/e_{ex}(0)$
\end_inset
 does not depend on the front: 
\begin_inset Formula $26.2\,\text{kV}$
\end_inset
 for 
\begin_inset Formula $d=40\,\text{cm}$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $27.5\,\text{kV}$
\end_inset
 for 
\begin_inset Formula $d=80\,\text{cm}$
\end_inset
. The length and the charge are then fixed too. In the paper the first-streamer curves decrease with the time to crest; reproducing that needs an inception criterion with a time lag, which the paper does not give.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Second streamer.
\series default
 Its inception voltage, length and charge all decrease with the time to crest, as in the paper. The second burst has to wait until the first cloud has moved away from the tip. On a slow front the voltage rises slowly, so the cloud drifts further before the voltage gets high enough (last column of 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "tab:repro_fig678_values"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
), and less voltage is needed. A lower voltage gives a lower applied field along the streamer, so the second streamer is shorter and carries less charge (
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{2}\propto L_{2}^{2}$
\end_inset
).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Plane.
\series default
 With 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:crossing_time"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $V=V_{p}$
\end_inset
, an ion needs about 
\begin_inset Formula $7\,\text{ms}$
\end_inset
 to cross the 
\begin_inset Formula $40\,\text{cm}$
\end_inset
 gap and 
\begin_inset Formula $15\,\text{ms}$
\end_inset
 for the 
\begin_inset Formula $80\,\text{cm}$
\end_inset
 gap, much longer than the longest front (
\begin_inset Formula $0.6\,\text{ms}$
\end_inset
) and the tail (
\begin_inset Formula $2.5\,\text{ms}$
\end_inset
). No cloud reaches the plane in these simulations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Length rule.
\series default
 For 
\begin_inset Formula $d=40\,\text{cm}$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{cr}=600\,\mu\text{s}$
\end_inset
 the dip of the head field at the apex of the first cloud (Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:bursts"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset
) falls below 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{c}$
\end_inset
. With the largest root the second streamer is 
\begin_inset Formula $6.71\,\text{cm}$
\end_inset
 long; with the first root it would stop after 
\begin_inset Formula $0.17\,\text{cm}$
\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The paper also shows measured values. A comparison of the values, and not only of the trends, needs the curves of Figures 6 to 8 digitized, as was done for Figure 3.
\end_layout

